  "comparative_analysis": {
    "core_finding_1_spatial_vs_non_spatial_incommensurability": "Direct comparison between Cohort A (spatial baseline Log Loss 1.3233) and Cohort B (Cricsheet non-spatial Log Loss 1.4282) is mathematically and empirically incommensurable. The spatial baseline consumes ground-truth wagon-wheel zone_id (79.7% coverage), which conveys powerful directional leverage unavailable in standard ball-by-ball scorecards. Comparing them without controlling for spatial schema falsely penalizes non-spatial models.",
    "core_finding_2_layer_a_vs_layer_b_separation": "Under Layer A, Multinomial Logistic Regression achieves superior probability calibration (Log Loss 1.4282, Brier Sum 0.6930, ECE 0.0125). However, raw argmax classification yields 0.0000 recall due to the ~5.7% rare-event base rate. Under Layer B operating-point optimization using tactical decision costs (policy v1.0.0), validation-selected thresholds (tau_w=0.2056) yield holdout wicket recall of 0.39% and precision of 8.28%, achieving expected tactical cost of 1.0312 runs/ball.",
    "core_finding_3_production_strategy_verdict": "Strategy C (Model Router) is the optimal architectural strategy. In spatial contexts where wagon-wheel coordinates exist, fieldiq_xgb_baseline_v1 provides directional intelligence. In non-spatial contexts (Cricsheet, real-time live matches without wagon-wheel), the router invokes the non-spatial probability model and applies Layer B tactical decision costs."
  }
